# Who owns each file in a repo scaffolded from this template, and which of a
# repo's two trees each one describes.
#
# `oxyc update <customer>` brings an existing customer repo back in line with
# `template/`, and the only question that matters when it does is which files it
# may rewrite. The live customer repos are not scaffolds any more: one of them
# carries 7 views, 4 topics, a pipeline, an app and five memory facts, and its
# `package.json` differs from this template's because the customer added
# dependencies for that app. A sync that overwrote what it did not own would
# have eaten all of it.
#
# So the answer is written down HERE, next to the files, where whoever adds the
# next template file has to walk past it. It was written a phase BEFORE the
# command that reads it, on the same bet as `.oxyc-template.json`: the
# classification is cheap to record now and impossible to recover later, once
# repos exist that nobody can classify from memory.
#
# lib/sync.sh is the reader, and it decides nothing on its own — every role
# below is taken from this file and `unmatched` is the default it falls back to.
#
# --- format -----------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   <directive> <glob>     one per line, whitespace-separated
#   # ...                  comment, and blank lines are ignored
#
# The glob is matched against a path relative to the repo root, with a shell
# `case`, so `*` spans `/` — `.github/workflows/*` covers any depth beneath it
# and `*.gitkeep` covers every placeholder wherever it sits.
#
# There are TWO families of directive and they answer different questions:
#
#   managed / scaffold / mixed          who owns the bytes
#   scope-repo / scope-workspace        which tree the file describes
#
# Each family has its own reader with its own allowlist, so a line from one is
# invisible to the other and a typo in either (`manged`, `scope-wrokspace`) is
# invisible to both — it falls to that family's default and fails the
# completeness test in tests/template_ownership.bats rather than quietly
# classifying a file under a word nothing recognises.
#
# --- the three roles, and what a wrong one costs ----------------------------
#
# managed   The TOOL owns the bytes. A sync may overwrite the file outright,
#           and a customer edit to it is a mistake that a sync is entitled to
#           undo. Classifying wrongly here is DATA LOSS.
#
# scaffold  Written once by `oxyc new` and never touched again. The customer
#           owns it from the moment it lands. A sync may report that it has
#           drifted; it may not rewrite it. Classifying wrongly here costs
#           someone a manual copy — which is why anything genuinely uncertain
#           belongs in this row rather than in `managed`.
#
# mixed     Both, in different PARTS of one file. A sync must merge, never
#           replace, and until it can do that surgically it must report and
#           leave the file alone.
#
#           That is the rule for a file that is THERE, and it is the only rule
#           `oxyc update` has: a repo the tool scaffolded was given both halves
#           on day one, so a mixed file missing from it was deleted on purpose
#           and restoring it would undo somebody's decision. Update also runs
#           over and over, and a command you run weekly must not quietly
#           recreate what you removed.
#
#           `oxyc adopt` meets the other case, and answers it differently. An
#           imported repo never had the file, so there is no customer half to
#           protect — and withholding it strands the managed files it pairs
#           with. The first real adopt did exactly that: pokehouse-oxy got
#           `scripts/dev.sh` and no package.json, so the `"dev"` entry that
#           runs the script was never created and `pnpm dev` could not work.
#           It installed the tool and withheld the handle.
#
#           So adopt — a one-time act somebody types on purpose — installs a
#           mixed file that is ABSENT and still never touches one that is
#           PRESENT. Per FILE, not per role: pokehouse has a .gitignore, and
#           that one is left exactly alone.
#
# --- and the role that is never written down: everything else ---------------
#
# unmatched  The CUSTOMER'S. A sync must not rewrite it, must not delete it,
#            and must not ask — never touch it.
#
# That is a DEFAULT, not an omission, and it is the single most important rule
# here. A scaffolded repo becomes mostly files this template never had: views,
# topics, pipelines, an app bundle, pnpm-lock.yaml, memory facts. Every one of
# them is unmatched BY CONSTRUCTION, and the file a sync does not recognise is
# exactly the file it did not create.
#
# So the safe direction is built into the shape rather than remembered: a rule
# has to be WRITTEN before anything may be rewritten, and forgetting to write
# one can only ever make a sync do less.
#
# `mixed` exists because of one concrete case, and it is worth stating: the
# root `package.json` holds a `"dev"` script entry that runs `scripts/dev.sh`.
# That entry is tool-owned — it is the entry point to a tool-owned script, and
# a customer deleting it has broken the local dev flow — while the
# `dependencies` beside it are the customer's app's, and are exactly what a
# blind overwrite would destroy. Two owners, one file. A two-way split would
# have forced a choice between losing the entry and losing the dependencies.
#
# --- the second axis: which of a repo's TWO trees does a file describe? -----
#
# A role says who owns a file's bytes. It does not say what the file is ABOUT,
# and in a repo `oxyc new` scaffolded there was never a difference to say: the
# repo IS the workspace, so every path above is simply "at the root".
#
# An IMPORTED repo splits that in two, and the template's files land on both
# sides of the split:
#
#   config.yml and README.md describe the OXY WORKSPACE. pokehouse-oxy keeps
#   its workspace in `oxy/`, where a config.yml and a readme already sit,
#   written by somebody before oxyc saw the repo. The template's copies
#   describe nothing at that repo's root — and writing them there would build a
#   second, half-populated workspace beside the real one.
#
#   package.json, pnpm-workspace.yaml and .gitignore describe the REPO ROOT,
#   and are not Oxy workspace files at all. The first two are the PNPM
#   workspace — a different workspace, which is where `scripts/dev.sh` is
#   installed and where its `"dev"` entry has to live — and the third is the
#   git working tree's ignore rules. All three belong at the root in both
#   layouts, exactly as the managed machinery does.
#
# That distinction had nowhere to be written down, so lib/sync.sh inferred it
# from the ROLE — `scaffold` and `mixed` were skipped together as "the
# workspace's" — and the inference was wrong for all three of the second group.
# pokehouse-oxy is what that cost. Adopted before the mixed-and-absent rule
# landed, it has `scripts/dev.sh` and no package.json; adopt then refused it as
# already-stamped, and update called package.json a workspace file and would
# not so much as name the gap. The file could not arrive by any path.
#
# So it is written down, on its own lines, in its own vocabulary:
#
#   scope-repo       describes the REPO ROOT. Always in scope.
#   scope-workspace  describes the OXY WORKSPACE. Out of scope in a repo that
#                    keeps its workspace in a subdirectory; identical to
#                    scope-repo in a repo that IS its workspace.
#
# A SECOND AXIS, and not a fourth role or a modifier on the three. The two
# questions are independent — package.json is `mixed` AND repo-scoped,
# config.yml is `scaffold` AND workspace-scoped — so a file that could answer
# only one of them would lose the other. A fourth role would take config.yml's
# `scaffold` away, which is the statement that a sync may report its drift and
# never rewrite it, and buy nothing back. A modifier (`scaffold@workspace`)
# keeps one line per file and breaks every reader instead: the
# `case "$role" in managed)` arms in lib/sync.sh, lib/adopt.sh and lib/doctor.sh
# stop matching a compound token, and in adopt_plan the fall-through is
# `continue` — a file silently not installed, which is precisely the failure
# this axis exists to end.
#
# THE DEFAULT IS `scope-repo`, and it deliberately points the OTHER WAY from
# `unmatched` above. A wrong role costs DATA LOSS, so the role default has to
# be the quiet one and forgetting a role can only make a sync do less. Scope
# cannot cost data: it never widens what a role permits, and `scaffold` and
# `mixed` are not written in any layout. What scope trades is NOISE against
# SILENCE — and silence is the whole defect. Forget a `scope-workspace` line
# and a file this repo will never have is reported on every run until somebody
# writes it. Forget a `scope-repo` line under the opposite default and the file
# can never arrive and nothing anywhere says so. The unwritten answer is
# therefore the LOUD one.
#
# tests/template_ownership.bats requires both answers for every file the
# template ships, so that default is unreachable from this manifest — it
# governs only a manifest already failing its own test.

# --- managed: the tool's own machinery --------------------------------------

# CI. The workflows and the shell they call are versioned with the tooling,
# pinned together (both files carry the same OXY_VERSION and a test enforces
# it), and a customer editing them by hand is how a fleet of repos ends up on
# eight different CI definitions.
# The globs below match file EXTENSIONS rather than whole directories, and
# that is deliberate. `scripts/*` would silently absorb a `scripts/NOTES.txt`
# somebody adds next year into `managed` — a file nobody classified, in the
# data-loss direction. Narrowed, that file is simply unmatched, which is both
# the safe answer and a failing completeness test until a human decides.
managed   .github/workflows/*.yaml
managed   .github/workflows/*.yml
managed   .github/scripts/*.sh

# Local dev. Same argument: `scripts/dev.sh` is the tooling's answer to "run an
# app locally", and it is meant to improve for everyone at once.
managed   scripts/*.sh

# The merge rule for the generated memory index. It is a statement about how
# oxyc's own generated file behaves, so oxyc owns it.
managed   .gitattributes

# This file.
managed   .oxyc-managed

# Not a template file at all — `oxyc new` writes it at render time, and
# `oxyc update --apply` re-points it at the commit it synced from. Listed here
# so that the sync's right to rewrite it is recorded in the same place as
# everything else's, rather than being a special case buried in code.
managed   .oxyc-template.json

# DELIBERATELY ABSENT: `.oxyc.json`, the repo's own statement about where its
# Oxy workspace is (see lib/workspace.sh). No rule is written for it, which
# means it falls to `unmatched` — the customer's, never touch — and that is
# exactly right twice over. It is hand-written, so a sync rewriting it would
# undo a decision somebody made; and it is the file that tells the sync what
# shape the repo is, so a sync that overwrote it would be sawing off the branch
# it stands on. Named here only so that the absence reads as a decision.
#
# This template does not ship one, either: a scaffolded repo is a workspace at
# its root, detection settles that unaided, and shipping an override would make
# the exception look like the norm.

# --- scaffold: the customer's from the moment it lands ----------------------

# Both are edited immediately and permanently: config.yml gains the models and
# databases of a real workspace, README.md gains whatever the team needs to say
# about this customer.
scaffold  config.yml
scaffold  README.md

# Node version. A single line, and the customer may legitimately move it — an
# app that needs a newer runtime is their call, not the tooling's. There is no
# tool-owned structure inside it to merge, so `mixed` would mean nothing here;
# a sync that forced it back would be an unrequested downgrade, so `managed` is
# wrong too. Report the drift and let a human decide.
scaffold  .nvmrc

# Placeholders that exist only so git carries an empty directory. Once a real
# view or pipeline lands beside one, the customer usually deletes it — and a
# sync that recreated it would be resurrecting a file somebody removed on
# purpose. Written once, then never ours again.
scaffold  *.gitkeep

# --- mixed: two owners, one file --------------------------------------------

# The case the third role exists for; see the note at the top.
mixed     package.json

# The `.gitignore` rules this template ships are load-bearing — dropping
# `node_modules/` or `out/` commits a build tree on the customer's next
# `git add -A`, and a test in customer-tooling pins the exact rule lines — so
# those lines are the tool's. Anything the customer adds below them is theirs
# and is expected: a scratch directory, an app-specific artifact. Both at once,
# which is `mixed`, not a judgement call between the two.
mixed     .gitignore

# Same shape, and the file says so itself: the `onlyBuiltDependencies` block
# carries the three entries the shipped app bundles need, with an instruction
# to ADD to the list rather than replace it. The `packages:` globs are the
# tool's; a fourth dependency that needs its install script run is the
# customer's.
mixed     pnpm-workspace.yaml

# --- scope: which tree each file describes ----------------------------------
#
# Grouped by answer rather than written beside the role above, so that the two
# axes are never read as one column of five roles. Every file the template
# ships appears here exactly once, the same rule the roles are held to.

# THE REPO ROOT. All of the managed machinery first: CI runs from the repo root
# in both layouts, `.github/scripts/` is what it calls, and the stamp and this
# manifest describe the repo oxyc adopted rather than the workspace inside it.
scope-repo       .github/workflows/*.yaml
scope-repo       .github/workflows/*.yml
scope-repo       .github/scripts/*.sh
scope-repo       scripts/*.sh
scope-repo       .gitattributes
scope-repo       .oxyc-managed
scope-repo       .oxyc-template.json

# The PNPM workspace and the git working tree, which are root things in a repo
# whose Oxy workspace is somewhere else. `pnpm dev` is run from the repo root;
# `packages: apps/*` names directories at the repo root, which is also where
# `.github/scripts/discover-apps.sh` globs for bundles; `.gitignore` governs
# what `git add -A` stages across the whole repo, `node_modules/` and `out/`
# included. `.nvmrc` is the node version all of that builds under.
scope-repo       package.json
scope-repo       pnpm-workspace.yaml
scope-repo       .gitignore
scope-repo       .nvmrc

# Two placeholders whose directories are also the repo's, not the workspace's.
# `memory/` is about the CUSTOMER and sits at the repo root in both shapes —
# lib/workspace.sh says so beside memory_dir, and it is the one directory oxyc
# creates in a repo it did not scaffold. `apps/` is where publish.yaml looks
# for bundles, from the repo root, and it is what pnpm-workspace.yaml declares.
scope-repo       memory/.gitkeep
scope-repo       apps/.gitkeep

# THE OXY WORKSPACE. config.yml IS the workspace — it is the file
# workspace_resolve looks for, and finding it one level down is what makes a
# repo a subdirectory repo in the first place — and README.md is that
# workspace's own readme, describing the layout below it. An imported repo has
# both already, inside `oxy/`, and its root README is the project's.
scope-workspace  config.yml
scope-workspace  README.md

# The placeholders holding open the workspace's own directories. They live
# beside config.yml in a scaffolded repo and inside `oxy/` in an imported one.
scope-workspace  pipelines/.gitkeep
scope-workspace  workflows/.gitkeep
scope-workspace  semantics/*/.gitkeep
